The primary sub-detector used to reconstruct particles in the analysis of the data from \(p+p\) and \(d+Au\) and \(Au+Au\) collisions is the Time Projection Chamber (TPC) [105]. Due to the size and acceptance of the TPC detector we use only events that come from collisions that appeared close to the center of the TPC detector. Thus, we require the z-component (along the beam axis) of the primary vertex to be within 30 cm from the center of the detector for \(p+p\) collisions at \(\sqrt{s}\)=200 GeV and 50 cm for \(d+Au\) collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\)=200 GeV and 40 cm for \(Au+Au\) collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\)=19.6 GeV.

